# Line endings are load bearing for the evidence set, not a style preference.
# results/SHA256SUMS records the SHA-256 of each captured log exactly as it was
# written, and check_result_markers_have_logs() in scripts/verification/ recomputes
# the digest from the file on disk. Under core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout
# yields CRLF bytes, the digest stops matching, and the gate refuses a result marker
# that the log properly backs. Storing text as LF in the repository is what makes a
# recorded digest mean the same thing on two machines.
#
# Line 1 used to read "text eol=lf" with no pattern token, so git matched a file
# literally named "text" and every real file stayed unspecified. Fixed as part of
# Issue #33 by adding the "*" pattern.
#
# Working copies already on disk keep their existing bytes until they are next
# checked out. Measured on the fix branch, not assumed: git normalises on read, so a
# CRLF working copy still hashes to the unchanged LF blob and reports as unmodified.
* text eol=lf

# Redundant under the rule above. Kept so the categories whose bytes have a
# contractual meaning are named explicitly rather than inferred from a wildcard.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.tex text eol=lf
*.lua text eol=lf
results/** text eol=lf
